# what: what git must never carry.
# why:  the first line is the one that matters. .env holds the model credentials,
#       and a key that reaches a repository is a key you must ROTATE, not one you
#       can delete -- git keeps history. .env.example is committed in its place so
#       a teammate gets the SHAPE of the configuration and none of the secrets.
#
#       The rest is run state, and Stage 5 added three more pieces of it: the
#       audit trail, the checkpoint file, and the demo trails. None is code.
#       Committing lab_checkpoints.db would also mean shipping a PAUSED RUN to
#       whoever clones the repo -- a gate waiting on an answer about a container
#       on somebody else's laptop.
# how:  verify before the first push with `git ls-files --cached`. A file that is
#       already tracked stays tracked even after being listed here -- .gitignore
#       only affects files git is not yet following.

# --- secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.local

# --- machine output --------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# --- run state, not code (Stage 4 memory, Stage 5 audit and checkpoints) ---
*.db
audit.jsonl
demo_*.jsonl

# --- the taught session's working files, kept out of the repo on purpose ---
output.bash
code.bash
CLAUDE.md
mvp_plan.md
